From b9f617a9aab6db54b2596bdd503cdb92b6db67d6 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Caleb Hattingh <caleb.hattingh@gmail.com>
Date: Fri, 20 Nov 2026 16:58:38 +0100
Subject: [PATCH] fix: coalesce destructive SSE2 scalar loop recurrences

## Big picture

Scalar Mandelbrot on x86-64 SSE2 copies three values on every backedge.
The loop's arithmetic can keep those values in their incoming registers
when the destructive operands and the preservation copy are chosen together.

## Changes

- Use a dying header parameter as the destructive multiply operand when
  the resulting scalar update feeds that parameter on the backedge.
- Delay an accumulator's square past independent arithmetic and pass the
  unsquared value through a block argument so register allocation can
  place the preservation copy directly in the next iteration's register.
- Test the allocated escape loop with the register-allocation checker.
  The optimized hot path contains three scalar copies and a direct
  conditional backedge.

Local Plenty issue #978.
